[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Tasks don't exist yet. How should we handle that dependency? → Include minimal task model (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the heart of it: what makes a task one a user "cares about"? This determines the data model, so it's expensive to
  change later.

  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're subscribed to tasks you created or are assigned. Zero UI, nothing to manage, matches what
    people usually mean. Can't follow a task you're not attached to, and can't stop notifications on a noisy task you
    own.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m — a "watch" toggle on each task. Total user control and dead simple to reason about. But nobody
    watches anything until they think to, so the feature is silent by default and feels broken at first.
  - [1mImplicit + explicit overrides (both)[0m — auto-subscribed as creator/assignee, plus watch/mute to opt in or out. How
    every real tracker works. Costs one more field and a bit of resolution logic (an explicit mute beats an implicit
    subscribe).

  I recommend the third. The extra cost is genuinely small — a per-user override that wins over the implicit rule — and
  the first option's missing mute is the complaint you'd hit within a day of using it. The implicit and explicit options
  are also not cheap to merge later, since the override needs to exist in stored data from the start.

  One thing I'm assuming unless you say otherwise: with no backend there's no auth, so "user" is a locally-chosen
  identity (a name picker, persisted in localStorage) and tasks carry creator/assignee fields. That's enough to make
  multi-user logic real and testable without pretending we have accounts.
